.TH CHTTPSVR_START 3 "2026-07-10" "c_collections" "C Collections Library - Functions"
.SH NAME
chttpsvr_start \- register a listener with the running engine
.SH LIBRARY
C Collections (libccollections, \-lccollections)
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
#include <chttpserver.h>

ccol_retval_t chttpsvr_start(chttpsvr srv,
                             const chttpsvr_config_t *cfg);
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.nf
Starts the server on the port and host specified in cfg.  cfg may be
NULL, in which case the defaults from CHTTPSVR_CONFIG_DEFAULT are used.

cfg->host may be a plain address ("0.0.0.0", "127.0.0.1", ...) for a
TCP listener, or a "unix://path/to/socket" string to bind a Unix
domain socket at that path instead (cfg->port is then ignored; a stale
socket file already at that path is removed automatically before
binding).  A single chttpsvr instance listens on either TCP or a Unix
socket, never both; create two instances for both.

If the shared, process-wide event_loop reactor (and its companion
idle-timeout sweep thread) is not yet running in this process, it is
started implicitly on this call and this call blocks until it is
confirmed running before proceeding.  This reactor is independent of
chttpclient's own engine; starting or stopping one has no effect on
the other.  The listener socket is then bound synchronously and the
function returns once the socket is in LISTEN state and registered
with the reactor.

Multiple chttpsvr handles may be started on different ports (or Unix
socket paths) simultaneously, all sharing the same reactor.

Port 0 is rejected with ccol_invalid_args, unless cfg->host is a
"unix://" path (where port is meaningless and ignored).

A ctpool for dispatching all handlers (buffered and streaming) is
created at start time using cfg->worker_thread_count threads and a
queue whose capacity is cfg->worker_queue_capacity (0 = 1024 * threads;
CHTTPSVR_QUEUE_UNBOUNDED = no capacity limit).  The pool is owned by
the server and destroyed in __chttpsvr_destroy.

Body size limit: cfg->max_body_size caps the request body.  A buffered
route whose body exceeds this is rejected with 413 before the handler
ever runs; a streaming route's handler is always invoked, and
chttpsvr_req_read() returns -1 with chttpsvr_req_stream_error() ==
ccol_msg_too_large once the limit is crossed.  Either way the
connection is closed after the resulting response (Connection: close)
rather than kept alive, since the excess body bytes beyond the limit
are discarded, not drained.

Body read timeouts: cfg->stream_read_timeout_ms bounds how long a
worker will wait for the next batch of body bytes (buffered or
streaming); 0 waits indefinitely.  Because it resets on any new byte,
a client that trickles data just fast enough never trips it, so
cfg->max_body_read_duration_ms (0 = disabled, the default) separately
bounds the *total* time spent reading one request's body regardless of
per-gap progress; see chttpsvr_req_read.  cfg->response_write_timeout_ms
(0 = use stream_read_timeout_ms's value) bounds the analogous per-call
wait on the response-write side, protecting a worker thread from a
slow-reading client during response send.

Connection idle timeout: cfg->read_timeout_ms/idle_timeout_ms are
enforced by the module-local idle-timeout sweep thread (shared by
every chttpsvr instance in the process), not by any per-connection OS
timer.  When idle_timeout_ms is non-zero it takes precedence over
read_timeout_ms; otherwise read_timeout_ms is used; 0 for both
disables idle-timeout enforcement entirely.  This only bounds time
spent idle between requests (awaiting the next pipelined/keep-alive
request), never time spent actively being handled by a worker thread.

Header size limit: cfg->max_header_bytes (0 = library default, 64 KiB)
bounds the combined size of one request's header block; a request
whose headers exceed this has its connection closed outright (no HTTP
response is sent, matching every other pre-routing parse error this
module's request parser reports).

Connection count limit: cfg->max_connections (0 = unlimited) bounds
how many connections this listener may have open at once; once at
capacity, new connections are simply left pending in the kernel's own
listen backlog rather than accepted and immediately rejected.

Socket options (all default false, all best-effort, all no-ops for a
Unix domain socket listener): cfg->enable_keepalive sets SO_KEEPALIVE
on every accepted connection. cfg->enable_reuseport sets SO_REUSEPORT
on the listening socket, letting multiple chttpsvr instances bind the
identical host:port for kernel-load-balanced accept. cfg->ipv6_only
sets IPV6_V6ONLY on an AF_INET6 listener, so it does not also
implicitly accept IPv4-mapped connections.

Restart: after chttpsvr_stop is called, chttpsvr_start may be called
again on the same handle to register a new listener.  The old ctpool is
drained and replaced with a fresh one.  Any TLS context from the
previous cycle is freed automatically before the new one is installed.
.fi
.SH RETURN VALUE
.nf
ccol_success on success.
ccol_invalid_args if srv is NULL, or if cfg->port is 0 and cfg->host
is not a "unix://" path.
ccol_not_permitted if this server is already started.
ccol_not_enough_memory if memory allocation fails.
ccol_unexpected_failure if the listen socket could not be created/bound.
.fi
.SH "THREAD SAFETY"
.nf
chttpsvr_stop only closes the listener socket; connections already
accepted before the stop keep running and may still dispatch further
requests on this same handle at any point during a subsequent restart.
The old ctpool is only drained and destroyed once every request already
in flight has completed, so a restart is safe to call even while such a
connection is still active; no request can ever be handed to a ctpool
that is concurrently being torn down.
.fi
